## Business Question

**Do month-to-month contracts and lack of tech support predict churn more than monthly charges do?**

Understanding which customer attributes drive churn lets a business target retention offers where they'll actually move the needle, instead of discounting broadly.

---

## 1. Load the data

```{r load-data}
 df <- read_csv("data/Telco-Customer-Churn.csv")

glimpse(df)
summary(df) 
```

## 2. Clean the data

```{r clean-data}
df <- df%>%
  mutate(
    TotalCharges= as.numeric(TotalCharges),
    SeniorCitizen= factor(SeniorCitizen, levels = c(0,1), labels = c("No","Yes")),
    Churn = factor(Churn, levels= c("No", "Yes"))
  ) %>%
  select(-customerID)%>%
  drop_na(TotalCharges)

#sanity check: no missing values left
colSums(is.na(df))
```
## 3. Cross-tabulate churn against key variables

```{r cross-tabs}
# Churn rate by contract type
df %>%
  group_by(Contract) %>%
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(churn_rate))

# Churn rate by tech support
df %>%
  group_by(TechSupport) %>%
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(churn_rate))

# Churn rate by payment method
df %>%
  group_by(PaymentMethod) %>%
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(churn_rate))

# Churn rate by internet service
df %>%
  group_by(InternetService) %>%
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")
  ) %>%
  arrange(desc(churn_rate))
```

## 4. Visualizations

```{r viz-contract}
df %>%
  group_by(Contract) %>%
  summarise(churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")) %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = reorder(Contract, -churn_rate), y = churn_rate)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#2C7FB8") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(
    title = "Churn Rate by Contract Type",
    x = "Contract Type",
    y = "Churn Rate"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

```{r viz-tenure}
ggplot(df, aes(x = Churn, y = tenure, fill = Churn)) +
  geom_boxplot() +
  labs(
    title = "Tenure Distribution by Churn Status",
    x = "Churned?",
    y = "Tenure (months)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "none")
```

```{r viz-techsupport}
df %>%
  group_by(TechSupport) %>%
  summarise(churn_rate = mean(Churn == "Yes")) %>%
  ggplot(aes(x = reorder(TechSupport, -churn_rate), y = churn_rate)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#D95F02") +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(
    title = "Churn Rate by Tech Support Subscription",
    x = "Has Tech Support?",
    y = "Churn Rate"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## 5. Findings

*(Fill these in once you've run the code above and looked at your actual numbers —
the structure below is a guide, not the final numbers.)*

1. **Finding 1 (contract type):** Month-to-month customers churn at roughly 15x the rate of two-year contract customers.

2. **Finding 2 (tenure):** Churned customers have a median tenure of 10 months vs.38 months for retained customers — churn risk is highest early in the relationship.

3. **Finding 3 (tech support):** Customers without tech support churn at 41.6% vs.15.2% for those with it.

### So what?

Retention offers should prioritize month-to-month customers in their first 10 months, and bundling free or discounted tech support during this initial onboarding window will likely yield a stronger reduction in churn than plain price discounts.

